.TH HIRZ 1 2026-11-16 hirz "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hirz \- set up and manage the hirz passkey vault
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hirz
.RB [ \-\-vault
.IR DIR ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B hirz help
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B hirz
.BR \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hirz
manages the vault that holds your passkeys (WebAuthn and FIDO2 credentials) and the setup
around it.
It never talks to the network.
Passkey requests are answered and approved by
.BR hirzd (1)
(the virtual security key) and
.BR hirz\-nmh (1)
(the Firefox extension host);
.B hirz
only manages the vault.
.PP
The vault is a folder of encrypted files that your own sync tool may copy between your
computers.
Each computer keeps its own device keys in the device state directory, which is never
synced.
.PP
Options take their value as
.B \-\-opt value
or
.BR \-\-opt=value .
.B \-\-
ends the options, so an account name that starts with
.B \-
can still be given.
.B hirz help
.I COMMAND
or
.B hirz
.I COMMAND
.B \-\-help
shows the help of one command.
Questions are written to standard error, so standard output stays clean when it is
redirected.
Recovery phrases, PINs and passphrases are read without echo when standard input is a
terminal; echo is switched back on when the question ends and when Ctrl\-C or another signal
ends the process during it.
.PP
Sites are shown by their relying party id.
Keys that OpenSSH created on hirz (relying party id \fBssh:\fR or \fBssh:\fINAME\fR)
are shown as "SSH key" or "SSH key (NAME)"; JSON output keeps the raw id, with DEL, C1
control, invisible and direction\-changing characters written as \fB\\u\fIXXXX\fR escapes
(a character beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane as the UTF\-16 pair JSON asks for, for example
\fB\\udb40\\udc67\fR), so the JSON means the same.
.PP
At start hirz sets RLIMIT_CORE to 0, PR_SET_DUMPABLE to 0 and PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, like
.BR hirzd (1),
so a crash does not leave a core file with the recovery phrase or the keys it handled.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-vault " DIR"
Use the vault folder
.IR DIR .
Without it, hirz uses
.BR HIRZ_VAULT ,
else the folder recorded by
.B init
or
.B join
in the file
.I vault\-path
of the device state directory, else
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/hirz/vault .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fR[\fB\-\-name \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\-i\-saved\-it\fR]
Create a new vault and set up this computer to use it.
With
.BR \-\-vault ,
the folder must be empty or not exist yet.
.B \-\-name
sets this computer's name as other devices see it (1 to 64 characters, default the host
name).
The 24\-word recovery phrase is shown once, on the terminal's alternate screen so that it
does not stay in scrollback, and three of its words must be typed back;
.B \-\-yes\-i\-saved\-it
skips that check and prints the phrase on standard output as it is (for scripts).
Refuses when this computer is already set up or the folder already holds a vault.
At the end, when Firefox is installed without the hirz extension, hirz offers to open the
extension's page on addons.mozilla.org with
.BR xdg\-open (1);
this step never makes
.B init
fail.
.TP
.B init \-\-gui \fR[\fB\-\-name \fINAME\fR]
The same setup in zenity windows:
what hirz is (Set up, join from another computer, or Cancel), the 24 words numbered in a
grid, three of them typed back, the Firefox extension, and the backup reminder.
The phrase reaches zenity only on its standard input.
.BR hirzd (1)
and
.BR hirz\-nmh (1)
start it when an app asks for a passkey and there is no vault.
Only one setup window runs at a time (a lock in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hirz );
after Cancel, requests do not open it again for a minute.
A cancel exits with status 0.
.TP
.B join \-\-vault \fIDIR\fB \-\-request \fR[\fB\-\-name \fINAME\fR]
Use an existing vault that your sync tool copied to
.IR DIR ,
approved from a device that already uses it.
hirz writes a request into the vault folder, shows four verification words and waits.
On the other device run
.B hirz devices approve
and compare the words.
The name in the request is
.B \-\-name
or the host name, reduced to 1 to 32 characters of ASCII letters, digits, spaces and
.BR "\- _ ." :
other characters become
.BR \- ,
words in capitals are lowercased, and a name that cannot be repaired becomes
.BR new\-device .
That is the name this computer joins under.
The request expires after 15 minutes.
.TP
.B join \-\-vault \fIDIR\fR [\fB\-\-name \fINAME\fR]
The same without another device: type the vault's recovery phrase.
.TP
.B status \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
Show the vault and state folders, this device, the number of passkeys and devices, whether
passkeys count as backed up, sync problems, the approval methods that work now, the Firefox
native messaging manifests and whether something answers on the uhid broker socket (a
socket file that refuses connections is reported as stale).
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-\-site \fISITE\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
List passkeys: site, account, creation date (UTC) and the first 12 hex digits of the
credential id.
.B \-\-site
keeps one relying party id (exact match, case\-insensitive).
Private keys are never printed.
.TP
.B delete \fIID\-PREFIX\fR | \fISITE ACCOUNT\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Delete one passkey on all devices after showing it and asking.
.I ID\-PREFIX
is at least four hex digits of the credential id;
.I ACCOUNT
matches the user name or display name exactly.
When several passkeys match, nothing is deleted and the list shows enough of each id to
tell them apart.
.TP
.B devices \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR]
List the devices of the vault, who approved each one (the name of a device, or the recovery
phrase), and say when devices are waiting for approval.
When more requests are waiting than are listed, say how many are not.
.TP
.B devices approve \fR[\fIREQUEST\fR]
Approve a device that ran
.BR "join \-\-request" .
hirz shows when it asked and the four verification words, and nothing the new device chose;
type
.B yes
only if the new device shows the same words.
When several requests wait, the list to choose from shows the words, not the names.
Only after that
.B yes
does hirz show the name the device gave itself, labelled as chosen by it, and ask whether it
is the device you are setting up.
A hirz window then shows the words alone, then the name, and requires user verification
(system password, fingerprint or hirz PIN).
.I REQUEST
is at least four hex digits of the request id.
.TP
.B devices deny \fIREQUEST
Delete a join request so that device cannot join with it.
.TP
.B remove\-device \fIID\-PREFIX\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Remove another device and change the vault key.
hirz lists every site the removed device could have copied, then requires user
verification in a hirz window.
.B \-\-yes
skips only the question in the terminal.
This computer cannot remove itself.
.TP
.B repair \fR[\fB\-\-discard \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-accept\-fork\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Show whether the vault folder reports a fork (the key changes this computer followed are
missing or were rewritten) or a rotation conflict (two devices removed each other), and list
the key changes this computer does not follow.
.B \-\-discard
deletes the named key change, as listed, and everything built on it.
.B \-\-accept\-fork
makes this computer follow the folder as it is now; it is refused unless a fork is reported.
Both ask a question in the terminal (a typed word for
.BR \-\-accept\-fork )
and then require user verification in a hirz window;
.B \-\-yes
skips only the question in the terminal.
Passkeys keep working while a fork or conflict is reported, but nothing new is saved.
.TP
.B forget\-local
Delete this computer's hirz keys from the state directory (device secret, key ring, event
counter and recorded vault location).
The vault folder is not touched.
.TP
.B pin set\fR | \fBchange\fR | \fBreset\fR | \fBstatus
Manage the hirz PIN.
.B set
asks for a new PIN twice (4 to 63 printable ASCII characters) and fails if one exists;
a PIN made of other characters, set by an earlier build, cannot match and is replaced with
.B reset
and
.BR set ;
.B change
asks for the current PIN first;
.B reset
removes the PIN and clears a lock, and on a computer set up with a vault asks for the
recovery phrase first (it is refused on a computer that another device removed from the
vault, as are
.B export
and
.BR import );
.B status
shows whether a PIN is set, the wrong tries and whether it is locked.
.TP
.B export \-\-cxf \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-plain\fR]
Write every passkey as a FIDO Credential Exchange Format document.
.I FILE
must not exist; it is created with mode 0600.
By default the document is encrypted with a passphrase of at least 12 characters.
.B \-\-plain
writes the bare JSON document after a warning that must be answered with
.BR yes .
Before the file is built, a hirz window requires user verification (system password,
fingerprint or hirz PIN), as for approving a device.
Passkeys whose site id
.B import
cannot read back are listed before the export.
.TP
.B import \-\-cxf \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Read an encrypted hirz export or a plain CXF document from another provider and add the
valid passkeys.
Existing passkeys are never replaced.
All three import commands list the sites and accounts they would add and ask first;
.B \-\-yes
skips the question, not the list.
.TP
.B import \-\-bitwarden \fIFILE
Read the passkeys of a Bitwarden JSON export, unencrypted or password protected (asks for
the file password).
Account restricted exports cannot be read.
Only P\-256 passkeys are imported; passwords, notes and other items are ignored.
.TP
.B import \-\-keepassxc \fIPATH
Read a KeePassXC
.I .passkey
file, or every
.I *.passkey
file in the folder
.IR PATH .
KeePass XML exports are not supported.
.TP
.B backup confirm \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Record that your passkeys exist somewhere other than this computer, so new assertions carry
the backed up (BS) flag.
.TP
.B setup firefox \fR[\fB\-\-nmh\-path \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR]
Write the per\-user Firefox native messaging manifest
.I ~/.mozilla/native\-messaging\-hosts/hirz.json
pointing at
.B hirz\-nmh
next to the running
.BR hirz ,
or at
.IR PATH .
An existing file that points elsewhere is replaced only after a question, or with
.BR \-\-force .
Meant for development builds: distribution packages install a root\-owned system manifest,
and a per\-user manifest takes precedence over it. When a system manifest exists the command
refuses and changes nothing unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.B help \fR[\fICOMMAND\fR]
Show help.
.TP
.B version
Show the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed (file access, damaged vault, nothing matched, already set up, this
computer was removed from the vault).
.TP
.B 2
Wrong arguments, or an ambiguous id prefix.
.TP
.B 3
hirz is not set up on this computer, or the vault folder is missing.
.TP
.B 4
Cancelled: the answer was no, input ended, or the recovery phrase check failed.
.TP
.B 5
A wrong recovery phrase, PIN or passphrase, or a locked PIN.
.TP
.B 6
Import finished, but some passkeys were skipped.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Base of the default folders.
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base folders of the vault, the device state and the approval config.
Relative values are ignored.
.TP
.B HIRZ_VAULT
Vault folder, as for
.BR hirzd (1).
.B \-\-vault
takes precedence.
Relative values are ignored.
.TP
.B HIRZ_STATE
Device state folder, as for
.BR hirzd (1).
Relative values are ignored.
.TP
.B HIRZ_BROKER_SOCKET
Socket that
.B status
checks (default
.IR /run/hirz/uhid.sock ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/hirz/vault
Default vault folder (synced by your sync tool).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/hirz
Device state: device key, key ring, counter, PIN verifier and
.IR vault\-path .
Never synced.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hirz/config
Approval configuration, see
.BR hirzd (1).
.TP
.I ~/.mozilla/native\-messaging\-hosts/hirz.json
Per\-user Firefox manifest written by
.BR "setup firefox" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hirzd (1),
.BR hirz\-nmh (1),
.BR hirz\-uhid\-broker (8)
.PP
The documentation in
.I docs/
of the hirz sources, in particular
.IR cli.md ,
.I sync.md
and
.IR threat\-model.md .
